# ///
"""Am I over or under on Claude Code this week? — and what to do about it tonight.

    ccpace              the card: the week, today, the speed, and what to do now
    ccpace -f           skip the 60s cache and re-fetch
    ccpace -w 30        redraw every 30 seconds (bare -w: every 60); r re-fetches now, q quits
    ccpace -j           print JSON and exit (scripting / statusline)
    ccpace --plain      the card with no box and no colour (for pasting or posting)
    ccpace --png FILE   the card as an image (for posting where text won't do)
    ccpace --strict     exit non-zero, with the reason, rather than show a cached reading
    ccpace --used 70 --now 2026-10-07T14:00    what-if, no network

The weekly meter is a single number that only means something next to "where
should it be by now". The plan below answers that: every day of the window gets
a share of the week ([days]), and every day spreads its share over the
clock ([hours]). A plan that puts most of a weekday in the 6pm-midnight block
has barely moved by 2pm and has nearly reached the bedtime number by 11pm.

Live numbers come from the same endpoint `/usage` inside Claude Code reads,
using the OAuth token Claude Code already keeps in ~/.claude/.credentials.json.
The token is only read, never refreshed here — Claude Code owns that file.
"""

